  "markdown_report": "### Insights and directions\n\n**Starting point.** (1) Terminal-mask supervision matches appended-MASK inference. Changing its example-level probability isolates objective allocation without changing BERT4Rec. (2) Feedback suggests broad historical relevance sometimes outranks current usefulness. This motivates testing prediction alignment, but does not establish that stronger recency weighting helps. (3) Existing geometry and margin probes describe training behavior. They cannot establish improved validation ranking or infer ownership, compatibility, or purchase intent.\n\n**Related work.** (1) [BERT4Rec](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690) already includes last-item masking. The contribution here is a controlled allocation experiment, not a new masking objective. (2) [Recency Sampling](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643) motivates aligning supervision with recommendation. However, [Measuring Recency Bias](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09722) cautions against equating recent-history dependence with better predictions. (3) [Gradient Surgery](https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.06782) motivates measuring objective conflict. Negative gradient agreement would indicate local tension, not prove that changing masking improves HR@10.\n\n### Framework\n\n| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed gap to test |\n|---|---|---|\n| Objective alignment | Random reconstruction and terminal masking | Prespecified terminal-example probability |\n| Context dependence | Bidirectional history encoding | Paired terminal/interior training probes |\n| Evidence quality | Margins and qualitative feedback | Separate measured ranking from inferred intent |\n\n### Candidate ideas\n\nScores are originality/potential/implementation difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n1. **Fixed terminal allocation: 3/7/2.** Set terminal-only probability to 0.75. Originality: clear controlled comparison, but an existing objective; potential: inexpensive and transferable, but possibly category-dependent; difficulty: small sampler edit, but requires corruption-bookkeeping checks. References: BERT4Rec, Recency Sampling.\n2. **Linear terminal curriculum: 4/6/3.** Increase probability from 0.25 to 0.75 across training. Originality: connects reconstruction and prediction, but resembles existing curricula; potential: may preserve early representation learning, but schedule sensitivity complicates attribution; difficulty: simple epoch schedule, but adds tuning. Reference: [Masked History Learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.23649), whose evidence concerns generative recommenders.\n3. **Gradient-conflict diagnostic: 4/6/5.** Compare terminal/interior gradients on matched training targets. Originality: task-specific diagnostic, but standard gradient geometry; potential: explains objective tension, but is not a ranking guarantee; difficulty: compatible with one model, but requires extra backward passes. Reference: Gradient Surgery.\n\n### Selected experiment: fixed terminal allocation\n\nAt 10% progress, choose the smallest attributable intervention: prespecify **p = 0.75**, provided it differs from the supplied baseline. Otherwise this is a replication, not a changed-condition experiment.\n\nFor each example, draw one Bernoulli variable. On the terminal branch, replace the final training item with reserved MASK and supervise only that position. Otherwise use the baseline random-masking procedure unchanged. Normalize losses exactly as the baseline does, documenting whether examples with more masked positions receive more weight.\n\nRetain one independently trained BERT4Rec per category, seed 42, at most 200 epochs, and 200 batches/epoch. Preserve splits, routing, predictor serialization, diagnostic interfaces, left padding, and final-nonpadding readout. Select solely by the host’s fixed macro validation HR@10. This tests supervision allocation; it does not directly solve compatibility or fulfilled-demand errors.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Prespecify terminal-only masked-supervision probability p=0.75 per training example, retaining the supplied BERT4Rec random-masking procedure on the remaining examples. Train exactly one model per category.",
    "motivation": "Terminal supervision matches appended-MASK prediction while preserving auxiliary bidirectional reconstruction. A single probability change offers an interpretable early-stage experiment; any ranking improvement remains an empirical question.",
    "implementation_notes": "Inspect the supplied baseline probability during implementation; if it already equals 0.75, report replication rather than claiming a changed condition. Preserve os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local artifacts, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, all four dataset routes, maximum 200 epochs, and 200 batches per epoch. Terminal examples must contain only the target's preceding history and a reserved MASK replacing the target; never append an unmasked shifted target. Use the final nonpadding index under left padding. Keep the baseline loss reduction, architecture, optimizer, and remaining sampler settings fixed for attribution. Preserve ModelView hooks and DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis); update exact sampler bookkeeping. Keep the predictor writer consistent with the checkpoint architecture. No checkpoints, scores, or independently trained components are combined. No experimental results are available in the supplied input.",
    "pseudocode": "p_terminal = 0.75  # fixed before validation\nfor category in FOUR_CATEGORIES:\n    set_training_seed(42)\n    model = supplied_corrected_bert4rec(category)\n    for epoch in range(configured_epochs_at_most_200):\n        for step in range(200):\n            examples = sample_training_histories_using_baseline_policy()\n            for history in examples:\n                if sampler_rng.random() < p_terminal:\n                    tokens = history[-maxlen:].copy()\n                    labels = zeros_like(tokens)\n                    labels[-1] = tokens[-1]\n                    tokens[-1] = RESERVED_MASK\n                    record_terminal_corruption()\n                else:\n                    tokens, labels = baseline_random_mask(history)\n                add_left_padded_example(tokens, labels)\n            loss = baseline_masked_loss(model(batch.tokens), batch.labels)\n            optimizer.zero_grad()\n            loss.backward()\n            optimizer.step()\n    save_one_category_model_using_baseline_checkpoint_policy()\nwrite_architecture_consistent_predictor()\n# Inference: retain last maxlen-1 observed items, append MASK,\n# left-pad, and score from the maximum nonpadding index.\n# Host independently evaluates fixed validation candidates.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "A clean task-specific allocation experiment can isolate the effect of terminal supervision.",
      "negative": "Terminal masking is already part of BERT4Rec; changing its probability is incremental."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "A controlled result can guide later curricula and matched-target objective diagnostics.",
      "negative": "Benefits may depend on category and baseline settings, and do not establish improved modeling of current intent."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
